---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// The method page is the product's credibility argument. It is written to be
// read by a sceptic, so it states limits as plainly as capabilities.
const components = [
  {
    name: 'Independent corroboration',
    max: 40,
    body: 'How many genuinely separate outlets carried the claim. Outlets under common ownership count once — eight titles owned by the same publisher reflect one editorial decision, not eight confirmations.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Source proximity',
    max: 35,
    body: 'How close the best source sits to the event. A company announcing its own model scores highest; an anonymous account reposting a screenshot scores lowest.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Internal consistency',
    max: 10,
    body: 'Reduced when sources contradict each other, and when a claim is hedged without any primary source behind it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Model judgment',
    max: 15,
    body: 'The only component a language model controls. Capped deliberately: a fluent, confident-sounding model cannot push a thinly sourced rumor into the high band.',
  },
];
---

<Base title="Method — Signal Index">
  <section class="head">
    <div class="shell">
      <h1>How we decide what to believe</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This site publishes claims about unreleased products, security incidents,
        and company strategy. Some of those claims are wrong. The method below
        exists so you can tell which ones we are confident about, and why.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="shell body">
    <section class="block">
      <h2>One story, one signal</h2>
      <p>
        When fifty outlets cover the same announcement, we collapse them into a
        single entry carrying all fifty sources. Grouping is done on the entities
        involved and the kind of event, so a launch, a lawsuit, and a price change
        about the same model stay three separate signals rather than merging into
        one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Confidence is mostly arithmetic</h2>
      <p>
        Every signal carries a score from 0 to 100. Eighty-five of those points
        come from countable facts about the sources. A model's own assessment can
        move the score by at most fifteen.
      </p>

      <div class="components">
        {components.map((c) => (
          <div class="component">
            <div class="component-head">
              <h3>{c.name}</h3>
              <span class="component-max">{c.max} points</span>
            </div>
            <div class="component-bar" aria-hidden="true">
              <div class="component-fill" style={`width:${(c.max / 40) * 100}%`}></div>
            </div>
            <p>{c.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="worked">
        In practice: a single anonymous post cannot exceed 35 even if the model
        finds it entirely plausible. A company announcement carried by three
        established outlets reaches about 90.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Nothing is published that we cannot trace</h2>
      <p>
        After a model writes a summary, every name, number, date, and quotation in
        it is checked against the source text it was given. Anything that does not
        appear in the sources fails the check and the signal is held back rather
        than published. Source excerpts are always quoted verbatim — they are
        never written or paraphrased by a model.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one exception is the section marked <em>Analysis</em>, which is
        interpretation rather than reporting. It is set apart visually on every
        page for exactly that reason.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Every category has its own view</h2>
      <p>
        The main feed carries everything. Each of the fourteen tracked
        categories also has its own page with its own confidence distribution,
        so you can read only rumors, only funding, or only security without the
        rest competing for attention.
      </p>
      <p>
        Categories are assigned by the same classifier that groups sources into
        signals, which is why a launch and a lawsuit about the same model stay
        separate entries rather than merging.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Rumors expire</h2>
      <p>
        A claim that attracts no corroboration within thirty days drops out of the
        feed. Claims contradicted by a credible source are marked debunked and
        stay published, because a correction that disappears is not a correction.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block limits">
      <h2>What this does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It does not verify claims independently. It measures how well attested they already are.</li>
        <li>A well-sourced claim can still be wrong. Outlets repeat each other's errors.</li>
        <li>Grouping occasionally merges two stories that should be separate, or splits one that should be whole.</li>
        <li>Coverage is limited to public sources. Anything that never surfaced publicly is invisible here.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head { padding-block: var(--gap-6) var(--gap-5); border-bottom: var(--rule); }
  .head h1 {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.1rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.12;
    margin: 0 0 var(--gap-3);
    max-width: 17ch;
  }
  .lede {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: var(--t-md);
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }

  .body { padding-top: var(--gap-6); padding-bottom: var(--gap-6); }

  .block { margin-bottom: var(--gap-6); max-width: 68ch; }

  .block h2 {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: var(--t-lg);
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin: 0 0 var(--gap-3);
  }

  .block p { margin: 0 0 var(--gap-3); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--text-dim); }
  .block p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .block em { font-style: italic; }

  .components {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--gap-4);
    margin-block: var(--gap-4);
    padding-block: var(--gap-4);
    border-block: var(--rule);
  }

  .component-head {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--gap-3);
  }
  .component h3 {
    font-size: var(--t-base);
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .component-max {
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .component-bar {
    height: 3px;
    background: var(--line);
    margin-block: 0.5rem;
  }
  .component-fill { height: 100%; background: var(--ember); }
  .component p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    color: var(--text-dim);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .worked {
    padding-left: var(--gap-3);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--amber);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
  }

  .limits ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .limits li {
    padding-block: var(--gap-3);
    border-bottom: var(--rule);
    color: var(--text-dim);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .limits li::before {
    content: '—';
    color: var(--red);
    margin-right: 0.6rem;
  }
</style>
